<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Document xmlns:p="property" xmlns:r="reflection" Id="RaTE04XdIRsb2s6149EGeK" LanguageVersion="2025.7.4" Version="0.128">
  <NugetDependency Id="DWcaH3grCjG4esnVdyXWNd" Location="VL.CoreLib" Version="2025.7.4" />
  <NugetDependency Id="Os7VxTyfTN4d6YhsoLsWgY" Location="VL.Skia" Version="2025.7.4" />
  <NugetDependency Id="Qnh6G0kq3f3Nj1ZfTQxGz4" Location="VL.Mapsui" Version="0.0.0" />
  <!--
    NetTopologySuite is declared HERE, by this document, and that is not the same as VL.Mapsui
    declaring it. A patch that uses NTS nodes itself needs its own dependency on the library, or
    the nodes below are "Not found". Measured in Tutorial 07, kept true here.
  -->
  <NugetDependency Id="HTLIbkV58Tnc4JBUrop3UY" Location="NetTopologySuite" Version="2.6.0" />
  <NugetDependency Id="CZjLFJs1YY8dEIks7FwB8a" Location="VL.NetTopologySuite" Version="0.0.0" />
  <Patch Id="LBwXs9zJoRUTxxur1nXgDa">
    <Canvas Id="Q7pXnwKPexnUm2z8E8Ks5K" DefaultCategory="Main" CanvasType="FullCategory" />
    <!--

    ************************ Application ************************

-->
    <Node Name="Application" Bounds="100,100" Id="AQDpXJU59pzl6KWNi7Ngof">
      <p:NodeReference>
        <Choice Kind="ContainerDefinition" Name="Process" />
        <FullNameCategoryReference ID="Primitive" />
      </p:NodeReference>
      <Patch Id="OIcWIJAcQAyzJQu7iaFUeT">
        <Canvas Id="KvHjx135pCMx1bdQ6Racvv" CanvasType="Group">
          <Pad Id="JQx8mb4PsLwnlNMbRji3cq" Bounds="-780,-120,640,1560" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="08 - The map is just giving you coordinates.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Open the renderer. AN ORANGE RING SITS ON TOKYO STATION - and nothing drew it but you: it is a&#xD;&#xA;plain Skia Circle, the same node Act I drew with, told WHERE by the map. Drag the map. The ring&#xD;&#xA;stays on its ground. Scroll. Still there. The map never draws it; it only answers, sixty times a&#xD;&#xA;second, the question 'where is longitude 139.7671, latitude 35.6812 on your screen right now?' -&#xD;&#xA;and the answer is two pixel numbers you can watch under PIXEL X and PIXEL Y.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;THE TILES ARE OFF WHEN YOU ARRIVE (Enabled starts false, as always: opening a patch is running&#xD;&#xA;it, and you have not agreed to network traffic yet). Notice what still works: the ring draws, the&#xD;&#xA;pixel numbers flow, the coordinates under your cursor update. The conversation between places and&#xD;&#xA;pixels never needed the pictures - switch Enabled on and the earth simply fades in underneath.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;TWO NODES, TWO DIRECTIONS:&#xD;&#xA;  ScreenToWorld   your cursor's pixel, answered as longitude and latitude. The map as a SENSOR.&#xD;&#xA;  WorldToScreen   a place, answered as the pixel it sits at. The map as a PROJECTOR for your&#xD;&#xA;                  own drawing.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;And under WRITE WKT, chapter 01 again, unchanged: Coordinate, Point, Write WKT - the same three&#xD;&#xA;nodes, but the numbers flowing through them are now places on the earth. Your cursor is data&#xD;&#xA;again. This time the data means somewhere.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;PLAY&#xD;&#xA;  Type another place into the two Place boxes. Some to try:&#xD;&#xA;    2.2945, 48.8584      you will know it when you see it (zoom out first!)&#xD;&#xA;    139.7454, 35.6586    Tokyo Tower - watch the pixel numbers while you drag&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;  Park the mouse and drag the map with a slow hand: the longitude and latitude under your&#xD;&#xA;  cursor change even though the cursor never moved. Which one moved - you, or the world?&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;  Zoom all the way out, then all the way in. The pixel numbers swing by thousands; the two&#xD;&#xA;  Place numbers never move. That difference IS the lesson.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;WHAT HAPPENED?&#xD;&#xA;  The map stopped being a picture and became a pair of questions you can ask every frame.&#xD;&#xA;  Anything you know how to draw - and after Act I you know how to draw geometry - can sit on&#xD;&#xA;  the earth now: wire WorldToScreen's pixels into any Skia layer, wrap it in WithinCommonSpace&#xD;&#xA;  set to PixelTopLeft so it speaks the same pixels the map draws in, and put it in the Group&#xD;&#xA;  above the map layer.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;GIS WORDS&#xD;&#xA;  screen space   pixels from the window's top-left corner. Yours, and gone next frame.&#xD;&#xA;  world space    longitude and latitude on the earth (WGS84 - the GPS numbers). Everyone's,&#xD;&#xA;                 and permanent enough to print.&#xD;&#xA;  projection     the arithmetic between the two. Web maps use Web Mercator (EPSG:3857);&#xD;&#xA;                 these two nodes do that crossing for you and hand you WGS84 (EPSG:4326),&#xD;&#xA;                 the coordinates every dataset in chapter 09 speaks.&#xD;&#xA;                 Honesty clause: this crossing is the ONLY projection arithmetic in the whole&#xD;&#xA;                 package family so far. The classic lesson - the same country in five&#xD;&#xA;                 projections, watching Greenland eat Africa - is planned and blocked: no&#xD;&#xA;                 reprojection node exists yet.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;CREATIVE CONNECTION&#xD;&#xA;  Unfolding, the map library written for designers, teaches this same node pair BEFORE it&#xD;&#xA;  teaches data files, and says why: 'the easiest method to create a custom style is to draw&#xD;&#xA;  the marker yourself.' This is the moment the map joins your toolbox instead of replacing it.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;NEXT - three directions, no order:&#xD;&#xA;  Tutorial 09      real data: many places at once, from a file or the network&#xD;&#xA;  Tutorial 01      the same three nodes before the earth arrived - see how little changed&#xD;&#xA;  Prompt A mountain    no basemap at all; the geometry IS the place">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">11</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="UVf2pWb39GNSAZyCyIjujI" Comment="OpenStreetMap" Bounds="-50,-115,430,15" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="https://tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="OQCrehktMTtsdPABjXNmAL" Comment="OpenStreetMap credit" Bounds="-50,-85,430,15" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="(c) OpenStreetMap contributors">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
          </Pad>
